Identity: the held Federal Register text (27 FR 5699) prints Executive Order 11029 as an order of the President (John F. Kennedy) dated 1962-06-13; the number, subject and date agree with the census register row (subject: Amendment of Executive Order No. 
	11025, creating a Board of Inquiry to report on a labor dispute affecting the aircraft industry of the United States). I read the whole clause and signature block. Inventory: the dossier's single whole-order clause covers every operative sentence; I confirm it as complete. The one operative clause amends the title of Executive Order No. 11025 of June 7, 1962 and substitutes new text for its first and second paragraphs: recitals naming the parties to the dispute at the Republic Aviation Corporation facility and finding that the strike affects the tactical fighter production industry. It contains no command of its own beyond that rewriting and no date or condition of its own. Ending: EO 12553 of February 25, 1986, titled "Revocation of Various Executive Orders", orders that the listed Executive Orders, and "any amendments thereto, are revoked:" Its table lists this order by number and subject on one line: "11029 Amending an order creating a board of inquiry to report on a labor dispute affecting the aircraft". EO 12553 contains no effective-date wording (the held text was searched for effective, take effect, shall become, except, notwithstanding, nothing in, provided that and saving, with no hit outside table subjects); it is headed as an order of February 25, 1986 and signed that day, so the date is read as 1986-02-25, the signing date. The alternative reading is the Federal Register publication date, 1986-03-03 (Monday, March 3, 1986, 51 FR 7237); no later date is supported by the text. The quoted source for the end is the enacting paragraph of EO 12553, which names no order; the order is named by its row in the No./Subject table, quoted above, so the table row and the enacting paragraph are read together. Because the order only rewrites the text of EO 11025, it has no performance date, report deadline or sunset of its own; the amendment was effective when made and the rewritten text then formed part of EO 11025. EO 11025 is also listed in EO 12553 (its row reads "11025 Creating a board of inquiry to report on a labor dispute affecting the aircraft industry"). I read the complete held Federal Register copy of this order (27 FR 5699). No later held order names EO 11029 other than EO 12553; the catalog notes name EO 12553 as its revoking order. The earlier order EO 11026 also rewrote the first paragraph of EO 11025 and is itself listed in EO 12553; its relation to this order is described in the claim on EO 11026 and is not an event for this order. Savings: EO 12553 has no savings clause or exception. Searches selected after reading their coverage notes (all three complete, no coverage warning): U.S. Code release point 119-121 (no hit for this order), eCFR as of 2026-10-14 (no hit), and the Federal Register catalog disposition notes (2 machine hits, the revocation note on this order and the matching note on EO 12553).
Member checks: {"earlier_end_check":"No held source shows an earlier revocation, supersession, expiry or termination of this order. The own text has no end. I read the complete held Federal Register copy of this order (27 FR 5699). No later held order names EO 11029 other than EO 12553; the catalog notes name EO 12553 as its revoking order. The earlier order EO 11026 also rewrote the first paragraph of EO 11025 and is itself listed in EO 12553; its relation to this order is described in the claim on EO 11026 and is not an event for this order. The first ending shown by held evidence is the express revocation by number in EO 12553, effective 1986-02-25. Whether the Board of Inquiry created by EO 11025 ended earlier is a question for the claim on EO 11025 and does not supply an end for this separate amending order.","independent_command_check":"The order has no command independent of the text it amends; it creates no board, duty, deadline or authority of its own. No other order incorporating it was found in the held texts.","savings_check":"EO 12553 has no savings clause or exception (searched as described). Any effect the recitals had on the parent order's text was part of EO 11025 and is addressed in the claim on that order; no residual right or duty arises from this order.","scope_check":"Single clause, whole order: the amendment instruction and the quoted replacement recitals. I confirm the dossier inventory as complete. EO 12553 revokes the order by its own row, and also reaches amendments of the listed orders, so no part is left outside the ending."}

EO:11741 (signed 1973-10-15; 38 FR 28809, Federal Register of October 17, 1973) directs each Federal department and agency of the executive branch to use the official American Revolution Bicentennial symbol on publications, envelopes, stationery and other appropriate documents to the extent permitted by law and deemed appropriate (section 1), permits one-color or three-color use (section 2), and provides that use of the symbol pursuant to the order "shall continue through 
D ecem ber 31, 1976." (section 3). Identity: the registered original is the Federal Register printing in jobs/fr-originals; number, date and subject agree with the census register. Inventory: I confirm the dossier's three scopes, sections 1, 2 and 3, as the complete operative text; the recitals are not commands. Section 3 fixes the period of use, so section 1 and section 2, which govern that use, end with it on 1976-12-31, recorded inclusive (the text says the use continues through that date); the exclusive reading is 1977-01-01 as the day the period has run out. Section 3 is itself the duration term and ends on the same date. Searches, chosen after reading their coverage notes (all complete, none with a coverage warning): the U.S. Code search (release point 119-121, citations of the order by number in statute text and notes; edition current through 2026-10-09) recorded 0 hits; the eCFR point-in-time search as of 2026-10-14 with GovInfo CFR bulk XML recorded 0 hits; the Federal Register catalog disposition-note search (1937 onward) recorded 0 hits for this order. I also searched every held order text (the Cruden held-text search, 33118 readable files) for the order's number; the only held texts that cite it are its own Federal Register printing. I found no held order that revokes or amends it.
Member checks: {"earlier_end_check":"Own terms give the only ending found: section 3's period of use through 1976-12-31. The held-text number search finds only the order's own printing, and the machine searches record 0 U.S. Code, 0 eCFR and 0 Federal Register catalog hits, so no earlier revocation, supersession or amendment is held.","independent_command_check":"Section 1 directs agency use of a symbol adopted by the American Revolution Bicentennial Commission and section 2 allows its colors; neither creates a continuing duty beyond the period set in section 3, and the order assigns no function to the Commission.","savings_check":"Publications, envelopes, stationery and documents that already bore the symbol are accrued completed acts; the order continues no duty after 1976-12-31.","scope_check":"Sections 1 and 2 govern the use of the symbol and section 3 limits that use in time, so all three scopes end on the date section 3 fixes; no held text amends any section."}

EO 12358 (1982-04-14), 47 FR 16311 machine text. Sec. 4(b): "The Commission, shall terminate one year from this date" (1983-04-14). EO 12415 (1983-04-05, held APP text and FR original) amended 4(b) to "The Commission shall terminate on December 31, 1983." Effective date 1983-12-31 is the as-extended stated date. EO 12376 raised membership only. Dossier gap on EO 12553 is the 1986-02-25 revocation, read directly: after the end.
Member checks: {"earlier_end_check":"Searched: dossier chain (2 events), EDGES (EO 12376, EO 12415, EO 12553 revocation 1986-02-25), held corpus for the Commission name (EO 12358, 12376, 12415, 12553). The only dated end is EO 12415 sec. 4(b) as amended; the 1986 revocation postdates it. No earlier end held.","independent_command_check":"No independent command: functions, administration and general provisions all concern the Commission.","savings_check":"Read the order: no savings or exceptions.","scope_check":"Eleven dossier clauses (secs. 1 to 4); every one concerns the Presidential Commission on Drunk Driving. Sec. 4(b) terminates the Commission and nothing survives it. The amendment EO 12415 is a clean replacement of 4(b)."}
Fixer note (2026-10-20): first_end and whole_order_end now cite the held official FR text of EO 12415 (EO_12415.txt, 48 FR 15117) as the source of the date; EO 12415 and EO 12376 added as later events from held official FR texts.

EO 12528 (1985-08-08), 50 FR 32389 machine text. Sec. 4(a): "The Board shall terminate two years from the date of this Order, unless sooner extended." (1987-08-08). EO 12604 (1987-08-05, held APP text) replaced 4(a): "The Board shall terminate on July 1, 1989, unless sooner extended." Effective date 1989-07-01 is the as-extended stated date. EO 12692 (1989-09-29) sec. 3(f) revokes the order as amended after the end.
Member checks: {"earlier_end_check":"Searched: dossier chain (2 events), EDGES (EO 12604, EO 12692 revocation), EO 12692 read (revocation 90 days after the end), held corpus for the Board name. No earlier end and no later extension held.","independent_command_check":"No independent command: all provisions create, fund or administer the Board.","savings_check":"Read the order: only \"unless sooner extended\"; no savings.","scope_check":"Nine dossier clauses (secs. 1 to 4); all concern the Presidential Board of Advisors on Private Sector Initiatives. Sec. 4(a) terminates the Board and nothing survives it."}
Fixer note (2026-10-20): first_end and whole_order_end now cite the held official FR text of EO 12604 (EO_12604.txt, 52 FR 29495) as the source of the date; EO 12604 and the post-end revocation EO 12692 sec. 3(f) added as later events from held official FR texts.

EO 13278 established the President's Commission on the United States Postal Service. Section 5 set a report deadline of July 31, 2003, which is a deadline and not an end. Section 6(b) states: "(b) The Commission shall terminate 30 days after submitting its report 
and in no event later than August 30, 2003." The fixed outer date of August 30, 2003 is the order's own first end, and it precedes the express revocation by EO 13316, effective September 30, 2003. The report-relative termination is an earlier possibility whose trigger date (the actual report submission) is not held anywhere in the repository, so it is disclosed and not recorded. Searches of held texts by the order's number found only the order's own copies and EO 13316.
Member checks: {"earlier_end_check":"Own terms: section 6(b) supplies a fixed outer end of August 30, 2003 (recorded inclusive; the exclusive reading is that the order ended when that day began). The report-relative thirty-day termination could have come earlier but its trigger date is not held; disclosed. Section 5 (report by July 31, 2003) is a deadline, not an end. No earlier revocation or supersession is held; EO 13316 (effective September 30, 2003) is later.","independent_command_check":"Section 4(g) (non-impairment of OMB functions) and section 6(a) (no private rights) create no independent continuing command; every other provision establishes, staffs, directs or reports from the Commission.","savings_check":"No saving provision appears in EO 13316 for EO 13278, and the order's commands were exhausted by the Commission's termination.","scope_check":"The inventory is the 20 scopes the packet derives from the Federal Register text of EO 13278 (67 FR 76671). Every scope serves the single Commission (section 4(g) and section 6(a) are construction and no-rights statements within the same order), so the Commission's own-terms end closes all of them on the same date."}

EO 13762 of January 13, 2017 (82 FR 7619) set an order of succession for the office of Attorney General (section 1), exceptions (section 2), revoked EO 13557 (section 3) and added a no-rights provision (section 4). EO 13775 of February 9, 2017 (82 FR 10697) enacted a replacement succession list and in its section 3 states: "Executive Order 13762 of January 
13, 2017, is revoked." Section 1 of EO 13762 runs only until at least one named senior officer can act, a standing contingency that does not end the order. The held corpus holds no earlier revocation or amendment of EO 13762; the first end of the order as a whole is February 9, 2017, although its section 3 clause was performed earlier, on issuance (2017-01-13). Section 3 of EO 13762 ("Executive Order 13557 of November 4, 2010, is revoked.") is a self-executing revocation performed when the order issued on 2017-01-13, so its clause has an earlier first end by performance; the other clauses end with the revocation of the order by EO 13775 on 2017-02-09 (EO 13775 has no effective-date section, so the revocation took effect on signing). A by-number search of the held order texts for EO 13762 finds this order's own text and EO 13775 (Federal Register and American Presidency Project copies), and no other instrument.
Member checks: {"earlier_end_check":"Own terms: section 1 operates only during a vacancy and 'until such time as at least one of the officers mentioned above is able to perform the functions and duties of that office', a recurring contingency, not a sunset. Searched held texts by the order's number: only EO 13775 and the order's own copies cite it. No earlier revocation, supersession or amendment is held.","independent_command_check":"Section 3 of EO 13762 (revocation of EO 13557) is a one-time self-executing command, performed on issuance, and has its own earlier first end; section 4 is a no-rights statement that ends with the order. Neither is an independent continuing command that outlived the revocation.","savings_check":"The surviving consequence is the revocation of EO 13557; EO 13775 does not reinstate it. The order of succession itself is replaced by EO 13775.","scope_check":"Inventory is 10 scopes (a lead paragraph, sections 1 to 4 and their subsections) taken from 82 FR 7619-7620. EO 13775 section 3 revokes the whole order, so every scope ends on the same date."}

Text and identity: read in full in the registered American Presidency Project transcription (jobs/app-executive-orders/output/texts/executive-order-8796-exemption-george-cook-from-compulsory-retirement-for-age.txt; grade mirror_transcription, acceptable_as_original false; standing note: original not yet held; replace and re-check quotes when an official copy is retrieved). Page check per jobs/claim-kit/AGENT_PROTOCOL.md (working-text paragraph), done 2026-11-04: the APP mirror page record with the same sha256 in jobs/app-executive-orders/output/MIRROR_DOCUMENTS.jsonl (Common Crawl capture 2025-10-05T15:30:25Z) has the heading "Executive Order 8796—Exemption of George Cook From Compulsory Retirement for Age", its date (1941-06-18) and subject agree with the held text, and its text_sha256 equals the held file's. Identity: the register lists this number with date 1941-06-18, signer Franklin D. Roosevelt and the title "Exemption of George Cook From Compulsory Retirement for Age"; the held text concerns George Cook and ends with the signature "FRANKLIN D ROOSEVELT" and the date line "June 18, 1941.". Clauses are quoted from that registered file, not from the unregistered clause-drafts copy.
Content: the order has one recital and one operative sentence. It exempts one named employee, George Cook (plate printer, Bureau of Engraving and Printing, Treasury Department), from compulsory retirement for age under section 204 of the act of June 30, 1932, 47 Stat. 382, 404 (U.S.C., title 5, sec. 715a), for a period of one year ending July 31, 1942. The recital says he "will reach the retirement age prescribed for automatic separation from the service" and asks that he "be exempted from compulsory retirement for age for a period of one year". The inventory is therefore one clause, the exemption. Extension reading: this order is the initial exemption; any later exemption of the same person would be a new exercise of the section 204 power with its own period and a later event, not an end of this order, and the packet's searches show none in the held texts.
Member checks: {"earlier_end_check":"No earlier end found. The only cessation source is the order's own period, \"one year ending July 31, 1942\". The packet's bounded number search of the held order texts (lookup scope: exactly held_texts(), clause-drafts excluded) found no other held text that cites Executive Order 8796. The Federal Register catalog disposition-note search (later-events:ofr_catalog, checked 2026-10-19) returned no hit for this order. The person-name search of the held order texts (namier:held_texts_person_name, checked 2026-10-28, whose leads list later or undated orders only) found no later order naming George Cook. Statute check (the type rule research/readiness/type-rules/TR-RETIREMENT-EXEMPTION.json lists a change in the governing retirement statute as a possible earlier end): held APP exemption orders signed after this one still rest on the same section, for example EO 9271 of November 17, 1942 (\"section 204 of the act of June 30, 1932, 47 Stat. 382, 404 (U.S.C., title 5, sec. 715a), I hereby further exempt the said George T. Summerlin from compulsory retirement for age\") and EO 9530 of March 14, 1945 (\"section 204 of the act of June 30, 1932, 47 Stat. 382, 404 (U.S.C., title 5, sec. 715a), I hereby exempt the said Harry F. Hawley from compulsory retirement for age\"). Their wording shows that section 204 was still the authority for exemptions after this order's last day, so it had not been repealed. No held source shows that any amendment of section 204 extended or extinguished this order's dated exemption, and a later general rule is an end only with a reason it extinguished the command (owner ruling 2026-10-28). The employee's separation, retirement or death would end the practical use of the exemption but is not an instrument ending the command, and none is cited in the packet. Amendments are later events, not ends, and none is found.","independent_command_check":"None found. The order contains one command, the exemption of one named employee; it prescribes no report, procedure, delegation, appropriation or continuing duty. The compulsory-retirement rule it excepts comes from section 204 and the retirement statutes, which are independent of this order, so when the exemption's period ended no command of this order remained to continue.","savings_check":"The exemption is exhausted at 1942-07-31; its accrued effects (service, pay, retirement credit) are separate from the order and are stated as transferred_or_accrued. No later exemption order for the same person is found by the name search.","scope_check":"Scope: one named employee, George Cook (plate printer, Bureau of Engraving and Printing, Treasury Department). The dossier inventory has one whole-order clause (characters 0-660 of the registered file, the recital and the operative sentence); the signature and date line follow it in the full 695-character text. Nothing in the text is outside that clause, so no inventory correction is needed."}

